   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "A curve is given by the polar equation \\( r = 2 + \\cos\\theta \\). Find the exact slope of the line tangent to the curve at the point where \\( \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         1,
         2
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Write the Cartesian coordinates in terms of \\( \\theta \\): \\( x = r\\cos\\theta = \\left(2 + \\cos\\theta\\right)\\cos\\theta \\) and \\( y = r\\sin\\theta = \\left(2 + \\cos\\theta\\right)\\sin\\theta \\). At \\( \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\), \\( r = 2 \\).",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Differentiate each product with respect to \\( \\theta \\): \\( \\frac{dx}{d\\theta} = \\frac{dr}{d\\theta}\\cos\\theta - r\\sin\\theta \\) and \\( \\frac{dy}{d\\theta} = \\frac{dr}{d\\theta}\\sin\\theta + r\\cos\\theta \\), where \\( \\frac{dr}{d\\theta} = - \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\), which is \\( -1 \\) at \\( \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": -1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "At \\( \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\): \\( \\frac{dx}{d\\theta} = -2 \\) and \\( \\frac{dy}{d\\theta} = -1 \\).",
         "mathjson": -1
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The slope is \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy/d\\theta}{dx/d\\theta} = \\frac{1}{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            2
         ]
      }
